w: Here, O my Lord, I see Thee face to face; Here would I touch and han- dle things un- seen;
w: Here would I feed u- pon the bread of God, Here drink with Thee the roy- al wine of Heav’n;
w: This is the hour of ban- quet and of song; This is the heav’n- ly ta- ble spread for me;
w: Too soon we rise; the sym- bols dis- ap- pear; The feast, tho’ not the love, is past and gone.
w: I have no help but Thine; nor do I need A- no- ther arm save Thine to lean u- pon;
w: I have no wis- dom save in Him who is My wis- dom and my teach- er both in One;
G2 G A | B2 E2 | c3/2 c/ c G | A4 | A2 G F | B E E A | G2 F2 | E4 |]
w: Here grasp with fir- mer hand e- ter- nal grace, And all my wea- ri- ness up- on Thee lean.
w: Here would I lay a- side each earth- ly load, Here taste afresh the calm of sin for- giv- en.
w: Here let me feast, and feast- ing, still pro- long The hal- lowed hour of fel- low- ship with Thee.
w: The bread and wine re- move; but Thou art here, Nea- rer than e- ver, still my shield and sun.
w: It is e- nough, my Lord, e- nough in- deed; My strength is in Thy might, Thy might a- lone.
w: No wis- dom can I lack while Thou art wise; No tea- ching do I crave save Thine a- lone.
